<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning to Manage a Club – Lesson # 1</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Start a Club!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning to Manage a Club</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Extract key factual information, such as dates, numbers, and quantities from a presentation. Interactive: Interpret and relay information in pair and group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can extract detailed factual information from conversations and presentations about club-related tasks, such as goals, timelines, and membership requirements (e.g., "What are the club's main objectives?" "When is the registration deadline?" "How many members are needed for the environmental clean-up event?"). Can interpret and relay key details about different club activities from discussions based on a presentation or lecture (e.g., "The types of clubs mentioned are the social, sports, and academic clubs."). Can follow a presentation on club organization and answer specific questions (e.g., "What date is the club fair?" "The club fair is scheduled for next Thursday.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification to activate prior knowledge and introduce key vocabulary and pronunciation.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing pictures of different types of school clubs (e.g., a debate club, a sports team, an environmental club, a drama club). Students are asked to share their immediate thoughts. The teacher asks, "What types of clubs are these?" and "Which club looks most interesting to you?" The teacher then asks, "Last year, I joined a sports club, and I made new friends. What about you? Have you ever joined a club?" to activate simple past and personal narratives.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary words for the lesson: <i>clubs, interests, activities, goals, members, responsibilities, organize, plan, participate, social, structured, leadership</i>. The teacher writes the words on the board, models their pronunciation clearly, focusing on stress for words like `participate` and `organize`, and contrastive sounds like /p/ in `participate` and /t/ in `team`. The teacher uses simple present tense to describe a club: "There are many types of clubs. I like this club because we can use any room to work together." The teacher also models imperatives for instructions like "Set goals," and "Let's plan activities."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary.</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you have a clear objective for a club, what do we call that? (Response: a goal.)"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a group of people join a club, what are they? (Response: members.)"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What type of club would you like to join? (Response: I'd like to join a social club.)"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario "Let's Start a Club!" through an authentic audio source.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of a short presentation by a school administrator or student leader titled "Starting a Club: Your Guide to Success." The register is semi-formal and informative. It includes details about club types, requirements, timelines, and responsibilities. The audio will incorporate target vocabulary such as <i>structure, roles, meetings, project, event, community, feedback, suggestions, participation, leadership</i>, and verbs like <i>manage, negotiate, coordinate, assign, delegate, evaluate, contribute, support, facilitate</i>.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Receptive: Extract key factual information, such as dates, numbers, and quantities from a presentation. Interactive: Interpret and relay information in pair and group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the presentation once to get the main idea. Teacher asks: "What is the main purpose of this presentation?" and "What kinds of things do we need to think about when starting a club?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Specific information):</b> Students listen again and complete a worksheet with specific questions.</li>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are the club's main objectives mentioned in the presentation?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When is the registration deadline for new clubs?" (e.g., "The registration deadline is October 15th.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How many members are needed to officially start a new club?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What types of clubs are generally popular at school?" (e.g., "The types of clubs mentioned are the social, sports, and academic clubs.")</li>
                </ul>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Activities focused on developing listening accuracy and reinforcing target language through structured practice.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for factual details, new vocabulary (<i>requirements, structure, responsibilities, roles, negotiate, coordinate, assign, delegate, structured, organized, goal-oriented</i>), and grammatical structures (simple present, imperatives, simple past) from the presentation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill Listening):</b> Students receive a transcript of the presentation with specific vocabulary words and numbers omitted. They listen to the audio recording a third time and fill in the blanks. This focuses on accurate listening for precise details (e.g., "Clubs need at least [number] active members and a clear [goal]." or "To manage a club successfully, you must [organize] regular [meetings].").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Information Relay):</b> In pairs, students receive different sets of questions related to the presentation. They listen to the recording again (or specific sections) and note down the answers. Then, they take turns asking their partner their questions. The partner must relay the information they heard (e.g., Student A asks: "What date is the club fair mentioned?" Student B answers: "The club fair is scheduled for next Thursday."). This practices interpreting and relaying information.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Discussion (Peer Check and Summarizing):</b> Students work in small groups of three. They compare their answers from the previous activities, discussing any discrepancies. They then collaboratively create a short summary (oral or written bullet points) of the "Top 3 requirements for starting a club" based *only* on the information they heard. They practice using simple present to describe requirements and imperatives for instructions on club management (e.g., "Set clear goals," "Assign roles").</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">13 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the acquired language in a realistic, communicative listening scenario to propose a club.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Listening and Club Proposal Discussion.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students form small groups (3-4 members). The teacher plays short audio clips (1-2 minutes each) of different students proposing club ideas (e.g., a "Young Entrepreneurs Club," a "Community Clean-up Project Club," a "Chess & Strategy Club"). Each clip contains details about the club's `goals`, `activities`, `target members`, and `initial requirements`. After listening to all proposals, each group discusses the proposals, using the target vocabulary and grammar to decide which club they would 'approve' based on the information heard. They must justify their choice by extracting and relaying details from the audio. They practice asking about club preferences (e.g., "What type of club would you like to join?"), negotiating roles (`negotiate roles and responsibilities`), and responding to feedback (e.g., "I would like to be the treasurer because I am good at math."). They can use simple past to refer to a previous club experience, e.g., "Last year, I joined a sports club, and I made new friends, so I think a club with team-based activities is important."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to gauge students' listening comprehension and ability to apply new language.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and a quick written exit ticket.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students collectively present their chosen club and briefly explain their reasons (2-3 sentences) based on the audio proposals they heard. The teacher observes their ability to extract and relay specific factual information accurately.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of factual information extracted from audio (e.g., dates, numbers, requirements).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 5 target vocabulary words (nouns, verbs, adjectives).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and intelligibility of spoken responses, including pronunciation of words like `delegate` and `coordinate`.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to interpret and relay information effectively in group discussion.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a slip of paper:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most important piece of information you listened for today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new word you learned about managing a club and explain its meaning."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson, identifying areas of success and planning for future lessons.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> Students consider:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about listening for specific details in the presentations today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I improve my listening comprehension for future tasks?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The warm-up effectively activated prior knowledge, and students engaged with the club theme. The presentation audio was at an appropriate CEFR B1.1 level, allowing for gist and detailed comprehension."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Students demonstrated good progress in extracting factual information like dates and numbers. However, some struggled with consistently using the new verbs (e.g., `negotiate`, `delegate`) in their relay tasks. More explicit practice on verb usage in short sentences might be beneficial next time."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"For the next lesson (Reading Comprehension), I will provide a reading text that reuses these key vocabulary words and focuses on similar club-related information, perhaps an article about 'Successful Club Structures' or 'Planning a Club Event,' to reinforce the content and prepare them for reading for details. I will also include a brief review of the imperative forms for giving instructions, as this came up in the club proposal task."</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> The next lesson will transition to reading comprehension, where students will read a text based on similar club management topics, utilizing the vocabulary and concepts introduced in this listening lesson to deepen their understanding of club organization and requirements.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
</table>